%[text] # Getting Started with Palladium DAQ
%[text] Palladium DAQ is a modular framework for laboratory data acquisition: instrument control, data logging and real-time graphing. It includes a library of Instruments (drivers) for common lab hardware - multimeters, source meters, lock-ins, temperature controllers, magnet power supplies and more - and adding new ones from the provided template is straightforward. A default GUI is included, but it can be swapped for another, or Palladium DAQ can run with no GUI at all.
%[text] **[Open the Palladium DAQ documentation](matlab:web(fullfile(fileparts(which("Palladium")),"Docs","index.html")))** - user guide and API reference.
%[text] ## Features
%[text] - **Instruments** - a built-in suite of instrument drivers, easy to extend with new MATLAB or Python classes based on the templates provided
%[text] - **Real-time plotting** of all acquired data
%[text] - **Instrument Controls** - logic and GUI elements for sweeps, scans, heater and magnet control, and other complex measurements
%[text] - **Presets** - .json files describing commonly used hardware setups and settings, to skip repetitive setup on startup
%[text] - **Data Viewer** - a built-in programme for viewing and comparing saved data files
%[text] - **Sequence Editor** - scripts of commands for autonomous measurement runs
%%
%[text] ## Quick start
%[text] Run this section to launch Palladium DAQ with its default GUI, and add a simulated Keithley 2000 multimeter (`ConnectionType="Debug"` needs no hardware). Then press **Start** in the GUI to begin taking readings.
pd = Palladium();
pd.AddInstrument("Keithley2000", ConnectionType="Debug");
%[text] Palladium DAQ can also be launched by typing `Palladium` in the Command Window, or with a Preset: `Palladium(Preset="Example")`.
%%
%[text] ## Requirements
%[text] MATLAB R2026b or later, with the Instrument Control Toolbox.
%[text] ![](text:image:9c3e) [Palladium DAQ on GitHub](https://github.com/MattCoak-Research/PalladiumDAQ) - M.J. Coak, University of Birmingham

